# Erdos Problem #99 - low-t pentagon train-star packing

**Date:** 2026-09-14  
**Target:** C-II, canonical full-core Type-II branch  
**Dependencies:** low-t pentagon sea; empty-pentagon trapezoid stability; audited stopped-train decomposition; audited bounded-face no-return/singleton-loop exclusion  
**Claim class:** proof-only structural composition awaiting independent audit  
**External literature:** no new import

## LEVEL I STATUS

`STILL OPEN`

The small-residual low-`t` pentagon sea has more structure than a population of local
source defects. Every good pentagon is a stopping face for five **distinct complete
stopped trains**, and its flat-trapezoid normal form arranges those five train directions
in a local `3:1:1` phase star.

The fact that a complete train has exactly two terminal face-side occurrences gives a
strong global packing theorem for these local train stars.

Put

\[
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3},
\qquad C_0:=2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.00042,
\]

and on a convergent Type-II subsequence write

\[
t:=\lim{q-k\over D},\qquad
\delta:=\lim{b-P_F\over D},\qquad
\kappa:=\lim{k\over D}.
\]

Define the low-`t` pentagon coefficient

\[
P(t,\delta,\kappa)
:=\pi+C_0-{5t\over2}+\delta+{\kappa\over2}.
\tag{TS.1}
\]

Assume

\[
R_F=o(D).
\tag{TS.2}
\]

Then there is a scale `eta_D->0` and a family of

\[
G\ge(P(t,\delta,\kappa)-o(1))D
\tag{TS.3}
\]

good pentagons with the following simultaneous train-level consequences.

### A. Pairwise train-disjoint near-60-degree hinges

Each good pentagon has two edge-disjoint leaf teeth. Taking both gives `2G` two-train
hinge objects. There is a subfamily of pairwise train-disjoint hinges of size at least

\[
\boxed{
H_{60}\ge {2G\over3},
\qquad
{H_{60}\over D}\ge {2P(t,\delta,\kappa)\over3}-o(1).
}
\tag{TS.4}
\]

For every selected hinge, the two terminal contact edges share a source vertex, both
are exact unit contacts, their complete train axes have projective separation
`pi/3+o(1)`, and the hidden third side of the leaf triangle lies in `(1,1+eta_D)`.

### B. Pairwise train-disjoint almost-parallel flat hinges

Each good pentagon has one distinguished flat-middle pair formed by its two consecutive
long-base contact edges. There is a pairwise train-disjoint subfamily of size at least

\[
\boxed{
H_{0}\ge {G\over3},
\qquad
{H_0\over D}\ge {P(t,\delta,\kappa)\over3}-o(1).
}
\tag{TS.5}
\]

The two terminal train axes in each selected pair have projective separation `o(1)`,
and their terminal contact rays at the common middle source vertex have angle
`pi-o(1)`.

### C. Pairwise train-disjoint majority triples

The `3` in the local `3:1:1` phase star gives one three-train majority object per good
pentagon. A pairwise train-disjoint subfamily has size at least

\[
\boxed{
M_3\ge {G\over4},
\qquad
{M_3\over D}\ge {P(t,\delta,\kappa)\over4}-o(1).
}
\tag{TS.6}
\]

All three projective train directions in one selected majority triple lie in an
`o(1)`-window around a common axis.

### D. Pairwise train-disjoint full `3:1:1` stars

Finally, a pairwise train-disjoint family of whole five-train pentagon stars has size
at least

\[
\boxed{
S_5\ge {G\over6},
\qquad
{S_5\over D}\ge {P(t,\delta,\kappa)\over6}-o(1).
}
\tag{TS.7}
\]

Within every selected star the five train axes are an `o(1)` perturbation of three
projective axes separated by `pi/3`, with multiplicities `3,1,1`.

These are packings in **train variables**, not source-vertex packings. That is the
point: the train closure matrix assigns one angular variable to each complete stopped
train, so train-disjoint local objects have disjoint column supports.

## 1. Good pentagons and five distinct terminal trains

The low-`t` pentagon-sea theorem gives

\[
{p_5\over D}\ge P(t,\delta,\kappa)-o(1).
\tag{TS.8}
\]

Fix a geometric tolerance `eta>0`. Empty-pentagon stability and the first Wegner jump
supply a threshold `theta(eta)>0` such that a pentagon of residual below this threshold
is isolate-free and `eta`-close to the flat unit trapezoid. Since all face residuals
are nonnegative and sum to `R_F=o(D)`, all but `o(D)` of the pentagons are good. This
proves (TS.3) for fixed `eta`, and the standard slow diagonal gives `eta_D->0`.

Every boundary edge of a bounded exceptional face is a terminal occurrence of its
complete stopped train. A complete stopped train has exactly two terminal face-side
occurrences. The audited no-bounded-return theorem, together with the singleton
bounded-loop exclusion, says one train cannot use the same bounded face at both ends.
Therefore the five boundary edges of one good pentagon belong to five distinct complete
trains.

It follows immediately that a fixed complete train can occur in the good-pentagon
family at most twice: once for each of its two terminal occurrences.

## 2. A reusable train-packing lemma

Let `A` be a finite family of objects, each object being a set of `r` complete trains.
Suppose a complete train belongs to at most two objects of `A`. Build the conflict graph
on `A`, joining two objects when they share a train.

For any object, each of its `r` train members can belong to at most one other object.
Hence the conflict graph has maximum degree at most `r`. A greedy independent set gives

\[
\boxed{
\nu(A)\ge {|A|\over r+1},
}
\tag{TS.9}
\]

where `nu(A)` is the maximum number of pairwise train-disjoint objects.

For the two leaf hinges below there are two objects per pentagon, but they are
themselves train-disjoint inside that pentagon. Therefore a train still belongs to at
most one chosen leaf-hinge object at each terminal occurrence, hence to at most two
objects globally. Equation (TS.9) remains valid with `|A|=2G`.

## 3. Two leaf hinges per pentagon

Use the flat-trapezoid labels

\[
l,m,r,a,b
\]

with `l-m-r` the long base. The two leaf triples are

\[
(m,l,b),\qquad(m,r,a).
\tag{TS.10}
\]

Their boundary edge pairs are

\[
\{lm,lb\},\qquad\{mr,ra\}.
\tag{TS.11}
\]

These pairs are edge-disjoint. They therefore define two distinct two-train objects per
good pentagon, with no train repeated between the two objects of one face.

Both contact sides in each object have length exactly one. Trapezoid stability gives
hidden side length `1+o(1)`, strictly greater than one in the actual triangle-free
source. By the law of cosines, the angle between the two contact rays tends to `pi/3`.
Thus the projective directions of their complete trains also differ by `pi/3+o(1)`.

Apply (TS.9) with `r=2` to the `2G` leaf objects. This proves (TS.4).

## 4. Flat-middle train hinges

The two consecutive long-base sides

\[
lm,\qquad mr
\]

belong to two distinct complete trains. They define one two-train object for every good
pentagon. At the middle vertex `m`, the two contact rays become antipodal:

\[
\angle lmr=\pi-o(1).
\tag{TS.12}
\]

Equivalently their projective train directions differ by `o(1)`. Apply (TS.9) with
`r=2` and `|A|=G` to obtain (TS.5).

This object is different from the leaf hinge: the leaf hinge is a near-60-degree pair
supporting an almost-unit hidden chord, whereas the flat hinge is an almost-parallel
pair supporting a near-straight contact chain.

## 5. Majority triples and full phase stars

The equality model has oriented boundary direction multiset

\[
\{0,0,2\pi/3,\pi,4\pi/3\},
\tag{TS.13}
\]

so projectively its multiplicities are `3,1,1` on three axes separated by `pi/3`.
Stability preserves this pattern up to `o(1)` angular errors.

Take the three terminal trains in the majority projective axis as one object. There is
one such object per good pentagon and it has `r=3`, so (TS.9) gives (TS.6).

Taking all five terminal trains gives one `r=5` object per good pentagon, and (TS.9)
gives (TS.7).

The audited bounded-face endpoint-simplicity theorem additionally says that two
distinct complete trains cannot join the same two bounded stopping faces. Thus the
pentagon/train incidence network has no repeated bounded-face edge. This is not needed
for the numerical packing factors in (TS.4)--(TS.7), but it removes a potential
multigraph degeneracy from later topology arguments.

## 6. Current-floor constants

At the current proof-only floor

\[
t_{42}
={4\over9}\left(\pi+\sqrt{\Delta_2}\right)+0.0013751
=1.8208886379794447\ldots,
\]

and after conservatively dropping the nonnegative `delta,kappa` bonuses,

\[
P(t_{42},0,0)=0.4944166723690961\ldots .
\tag{TS.14}
\]

Therefore the small-residual low-`t` branch contains at least

\[
\boxed{{H_{60}\over D}\ge0.3296111149127307\ldots-o(1),}
\tag{TS.15}
\]

pairwise train-disjoint near-60-degree hinges,

\[
\boxed{{H_0\over D}\ge0.1648055574563653\ldots-o(1),}
\tag{TS.16}
\]

pairwise train-disjoint almost-parallel flat hinges,

\[
\boxed{{M_3\over D}\ge0.1236041680922740\ldots-o(1),}
\tag{TS.17}
\]

pairwise train-disjoint majority triples, and

\[
\boxed{{S_5\over D}\ge0.0824027787281826\ldots-o(1),}
\tag{TS.18}
\]

pairwise train-disjoint full `3:1:1` phase stars.

The selected leaf-hinge packing alone uses

\[
2H_{60}\ge0.6592222298254615\ldots D-o(D)
\tag{TS.19}
\]

distinct complete train variables.

For comparison, on any such subsequence with `t<=1.8585`, the conservative pentagon
floor is `P>=0.4003882673177079...`, giving

\[
{H_{60}\over D}\ge0.2669255115451386\ldots-o(1),
\]

\[
{H_0\over D}\ge0.1334627557725693\ldots-o(1),
\]

\[
{M_3\over D}\ge0.1000970668294269\ldots-o(1),
\]

and

\[
{S_5\over D}\ge0.0667313778862846\ldots-o(1).
\tag{TS.20}
\]

## 7. Frontier effect

The low-`t`, small-residual corner is therefore not merely pentagon-rich. It contains a
large collection of disjoint **train-coordinate** gadgets:

```text
0.3296 D disjoint near-60-degree two-train hinges,
0.1648 D disjoint almost-parallel two-train hinges,
0.1236 D disjoint coherent majority triples,
0.0824 D disjoint full 3:1:1 train stars,
```

at the current `t42` profile.

This aligns directly with the train-angle closure formulation: each packed gadget has
disjoint train columns, while the global diameter system must lock all non-Euclidean
train-angle motions. A useful next target is therefore a **block-local locking bound**:
show that one actual diameter row can lock only boundedly many train-disjoint flat or
60-degree hinges after the face-closure equations are imposed. Any bound below the
packing density forced above would convert these local objects into a global rigidity
contradiction.

A second concrete target is the conflict-cycle obstruction for the flat hinges. Their
conflict graph has maximum degree two. A triangle component would consist of three
bounded pentagons joined cyclically by three almost-parallel complete trains. Excluding
such same-phase train triangles would improve the packing factor in (TS.5) from `1/3`
to `1/2` on all noncyclic components and isolate a sharply defined residual topology.

No exact unit triangle or strict sub-`D` source is produced here. C-II and Level I
remain open.

## 8. Scope audit

- The theorem requires both the proof-only low-`t` pentagon theorem and `R_F=o(D)`.
- The five train occurrences on one good pentagon are distinct by bounded-face
  no-return plus the singleton bounded-loop exclusion.
- Each complete train is used by at most two good pentagons because it has exactly two
  terminal face-side occurrences.
- The two leaf hinges of one pentagon are train-disjoint; this is what permits the
  `2G/3` factor rather than `G/3`.
- Pairwise train-disjoint does not mean source-vertex-disjoint. The theorem is designed
  for train-coordinate arguments, not direct source surgery.
- The direction claims are asymptotic and come from the empty-pentagon stability
  theorem; no exact `pi/3` or exact parallelism is asserted at finite `D`.
- C-II remains open.
